    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4f215b57-4eeb-548d-a835-3e1b867939dd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,E=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $CD=DC={I}_{3}$\n- **II.** $CE=EC={I}_{3}$\n- **III.** $E={C}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- First, we check whether $CD=DC={I}_{3}.$ Computing $CD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $DC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $CE=EC={I}_{3}.$ Computing $CE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CE & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\u2217 & \u2217 & \u2217 \\\\ \u2217 & 5 & \u2217 \\\\ \u2217 & \u2217 & \u2217\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $E\\ne {C}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "aa932c24-06f7-503c-8a48-e9ddaaf80045",
